Q5 – Race Conditions in a Cinema Seat-Booking System (6 Marks)
This question focuses on race conditions, synchronization, critical sections, intrinsic locks and thread safety in Java.

The cinema booking system has 10 seats and 100 users. Every 10 users compete for the same seat. The main objective is to ensure that each seat is sold only once.
1. Shared Group Work
A. Version 1 – Without Synchronization
In this version, multiple threads can check and book the same seat simultaneously.

public class SeatBooking {

    private final boolean[] booked;
    private int bookingsMade = 0;

    SeatBooking(int seats) {
        booked = new boolean[seats];
    }

    public void book(int seat) {

        if (!booked[seat]) {

            // Simulate delay to increase race condition
            try {
                Thread.sleep(10);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            booked[seat] = true;
            bookingsMade++;

            System.out.println("Seat " + seat + " booked");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        SeatBooking screen = new SeatBooking(10);
        Thread[] users = new Thread[100];

        for (int i = 0; i < users.length; i++) {

            final int seat = i % 10;

            users[i] = new Thread(() -> screen.book(seat));
            users[i].start();
        }

        for (Thread t : users)
            t.join();

        System.out.println("Seats sold: " + screen.bookingsMade);
    }
}



Why does double booking happen?
Consider two users trying to book seat 1.
1. Thread A checks whether seat 1 is available.
2. Thread A sees that the seat is free.
3. Thread B also checks seat 1.
4. Thread B also sees that the seat is free.
5. Thread A books the seat.
6. Thread B also books the same seat.
Both threads increment bookingsMade.

Therefore, the same seat can be counted as sold more than once.
Possible output
Seat 1 booked
Seat 1 booked
Seat 2 booked
Seat 2 booked
...
Seats sold: 20


This is only an illustrative possible output. The actual result depends on thread scheduling.
