        for (Thread t : users)
            t.join();

        System.out.println("Seats sold: " + screen.bookingsMade);
    }
}



Expected output
Seats sold: 10



Explanation: The synchronized method allows only one user to execute the booking operation at a time. Once a seat is booked, other users cannot book it again.
C. Version 3 – Synchronized Block
Instead of locking the entire method, we can synchronize only the critical section.
public void book(int seat) {

    synchronized (this) {

        if (!booked[seat]) {
            booked[seat] = true;
            bookingsMade++;

            System.out.println("Seat " + seat + " booked");
        }
    }
}


Explanation:
- synchronized(this) locks the current SeatBooking object.
- Only one thread can enter the synchronized block on that object at a time.
- Other threads must wait until the lock is released.
- The check and booking operations become atomic with respect to other threads using the same lock.
Expected output:
Seats sold: 10


Comparison of the three versions
Version	Synchronization	Double booking
Without synchronization	No lock	Possible
Synchronized method	Entire method locked	Prevented
Synchronized block	Critical section locked	Prevented


2. Student A – Producing and Explaining the Race
A. Design a test to reproduce double booking
Use the following conditions:
- 10 seats.
- 100 users.
- Every 10 users compete for one seat.
- Add a small delay between checking and booking in the unsynchronized version.
The delay increases the possibility that multiple threads will read the same seat as available before any thread books it.

For a more controlled experiment, use a CyclicBarrier with 10 participants per seat. Each group checks the seat before proceeding past the barrier. This makes all 10 users for a seat observe the initial free state before any can mark it booked.

The following is a demonstration of that experiment:
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class RaceTest {

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        boolean[] booked = new boolean[10];
        int[] count = new int[10];

        CyclicBarrier[] barriers = new CyclicBarrier[10];

        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
            barriers[i] = new CyclicBarrier(10);

        Thread[] users = new Thread[100];

        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {

            final int seat = i % 10;

            users[i] = new Thread(() -> {

                boolean available = !booked[seat];

                try {
                    barriers[seat].await();
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    return;
                }

                if (available) {
                    booked[seat] = true;
                    count[seat]++;
                }
            });

            users[i].start();
        }

        for (Thread t : users)
            t.join();

        int total = 0;

        for (int c : count)
            total += c;

        System.out.println("Booking attempts accepted: " + total);
    }
}


Expected output:
Booking attempts accepted: 100


This controlled test deliberately demonstrates the race: all 100 users observe their respective seats as free before proceeding.
